049994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186054669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1031 "Horizontal and Vertical Lines" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1033 97 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050763 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "64c4dbd5-509e-506d-ad70-5a35f835770e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1031 "Infinite Limits from Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1033 1814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051049 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7d21c9de-358a-52ec-b910-f6117a085d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128914 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4012" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128914 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following is true when $x\\rightarrow 2$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$\n2. $f(x) \\rightarrow 2$\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty$\n\n![](images/33/33650d66a0f3a2fae762b1c19da952597740868b00eeec11a14ad822536962e8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ad1be49-273c-572d-8221-9605bfa16b7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128914 #edb/ref 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/45/4585f983dac68f3ac655fbaaed6481cd1479c4cffae2c10d9f08d991c6533f51.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128919 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128919 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d5/d5750d5909f8a6e7c893109dd2ebe1e6317e7e5505df52d8be8966b8f914f140.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is true when $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$?\n\n1. $f(x) \\rightarrow -2$\n2. $f(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$\n3. $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d4d4b06-ace1-5d4a-828a-decea7c5b5fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128919 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $x\\rightarrow 1^{+}$ means that $x$ approaches $1$ *from the right*.\n\n![](images/99/99421620750831182ef45a4cfda34ee4b36f772f88df5572783bffc3ea9580c5.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow 1^{+}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128920 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128920 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the graph of $y = f(x)$ shown below. What is the equation of its vertical asymptote?\n\n![](images/63/63fe6fd9a6a830b4f3f446d265ae57c24bec2e5f23917c4a5dda4000c63198e8.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128920 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dee2a1e-7dd9-5c99-9f3b-5189360a2ef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128920 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that $x = a$ is called a vertical asymptote of $y = f(x)$ if $f(x) \\to \\pm \\infty$ as $x \\to a^+$ or $x \\to a^-$.\n\n![](images/59/596435060b73c6df3b05bf5f56b4650c2e77ecb70203f3d680ad9d0dddc1f027.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the left, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$. Also, notice that as $x$ approaches $-1$ from the right, the values of $f(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\nSo, we can write:\n\n- $f(x) \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -1^-$\n- $f(x) \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to -1^+$\n\nThese give us the same asymptote $x = -1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128921 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128921 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8a/8a28f89ca66be27ba85ded32668b7077cb2b152f9877fe52f6702a40be047773.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Find the value of $b-a$, given the following:\n\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow -\\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow a$\n- $g(x)\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow b$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6212c1b-c1c8-5d0d-9984-1509b721e54d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128921 #edb/ref 1074 "From the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-1$, the values of $g(x)$ decrease, approaching $-\\infty$.\n\n![](images/c5/c5b5723c08edacff5a29e160b225343f565d23bb764a84149ca20ebbe28d4ad3.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to -1$.\n\nTherefore, $a = -1$.\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1$, the values of $g(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bf9f0d83f05002952d531fe1dcaa6ad14d7d7c4216a59d9a2d3a4e621b57ef5.png)\n\nSo, we can write\n$g(x) \\to \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\to 1$.\n\nTherefore, $b =1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb - a = 1 - (-1) = 2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129098 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129098 #edb/ref 1039 "Classifying Infinite Behavior of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129098 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129098 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d5dab76a-9d0c-5625-bba5-f903a55e3b7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129103 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129103 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129103 #edb/ref 1039 "Recognizing One-Sided Infinite Behavior of a Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129103 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129103 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "408f96cb-5b2d-532b-874b-f9441dddfa30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Vertical Asymptotes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129104 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6b81a25e-32d1-5f5f-9f8e-b18e5d354e2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Points Where a Function Approaches Infinity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129105 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d29ea673-5a84-5e48-b45d-85cade99566d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1045 "Unbounded Behavior of Functions Near a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129379 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "35ebca4d-3a0a-5ee7-ba60-31eab0fe08b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129918 #edb/ref 1061 14196 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30bb5032-8b6d-589e-b245-21d8afdfc337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129919 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129919 #edb/ref 1061 14204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129919 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e92dff94-995f-5d19-8b28-bee497c247ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129919 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129924 #edb/ref 1061 14365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "add53e6f-c711-5389-b34a-63cf0fb453b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129925 #edb/ref 1061 14366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa841aed-7d9e-52b6-a301-5ea7cab278ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129926 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129926 #edb/ref 1061 14367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129926 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f39e32c5-50b4-5125-8c49-cf5bb0d9a1c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129926 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129927 #edb/ref 1061 14434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e1906c7-3dc3-5800-8af2-7c02ee80bf1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129934 #edb/ref 1061 15203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c74a9040-1f67-519b-98bd-8fa831f5eddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130335 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, a function might increase or decrease without bound when its input gets close to a specific value.\n\nFor example, consider the function whose graph is shown below:\n\n![](images/21/2163d96abc422e376581a693a6270da9d45ccc79d426d89f7ba9d1b046e2a254.png)\n\nAs the input $x$ gets closer to $1$, the values of the function get larger and larger.\n\n![](images/c3/c367d69e8ef7369e81872f6f78460d8a2052a35e31d8f46e2b072d24a6d83f5a.png)\n\nWhen this happens, we say that the function approaches \"infinity\", or that it has an **infinite limit** at $x=1$. We can write this mathematically, as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty\\quad \\text{as}\\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $f(x)$ approaches infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\".\n\nNow, consider the function $y = g(x)$, shown below. This is an example of a function that *decreases* without bound.\n\n![](images/a2/a21c625314d96dca5e724064bc35e67bed7c61fd56acc993cd7d281da29a2062.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$. So, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 1\n$$\n\nIn words, this reads \"the function $g(x)$ approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $1$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130335 #edb/ref 1065 2781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130335 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "993e05cc-872c-5156-9300-df403918128f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130335 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130336 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that some functions approach infinity as $x$ approaches a certain value, like the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/da/da7878b503b9dff8f04c0f2c42e021353dc8b5fa744476fa0227bb954472c5ed.png)\n\nHere, the function shoots upwards when $x$ approaches $2$ *from the right*, that is, from the positive direction of the $x$-axis.\n\nWe say that the function approaches positive infinity as $x$ approaches $2$ from the right, and we write\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 2^+\n$$\n\nwhere the superscript $+$ next to the $2$ indicates that $x$ approaches $2$ from the right.\n\nSimilar to the case above, we can have a function that shoots upwards or downwards when $x$ approaches a finite value *from the left*, that is, from the negative direction of the $x$-axis. Consider the function $y=g(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/2c/2c3d79df60e6b0a5627f39e8397a1eb9dc6c5e48fb1e816f6adc5309545ffb29.png)\n\nWe say that the function approaches negative infinity as $x$ approaches $2$ from the left, and we write\n\n$$\ng(x) \\rightarrow -\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow 2^-\n$$\n\nwhere the superscript $-$ indicates that $x$ approaches $2$ from the left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130336 #edb/ref 1065 2790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130336 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d16be94-aa13-52a0-bb7a-333c0a9d03ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130336 #edb/ref 1067 "One-Sided Infinite Behavior of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130338 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that some functions approach infinity as $x$ approaches a certain value from one side, like the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ plotted below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b1633611a2ea867e44845a8ba484e253671b9d5c74629f4a153ea79cf9a62e0.png)\n\nWe call the line $x=2$ a **vertical asymptote** of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$.\n\nAn asymptote is a line that a function gets very close to but never touches. In general, if a function $f(x)$ has an infinite limit (positive or negative) at a point $a$, then we write\n\n$$\nf(x) \\rightarrow \\pm \\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow a^+\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nf(x)\\rightarrow \\pm\\infty \\quad \\text{as} \\quad x \\rightarrow a^-\n$$\n\nand $x=a$ is a vertical asymptote of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130338 #edb/ref 1065 2855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130338 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d3254a6-2a2f-5033-96a8-e1c1c6f2b8d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130338 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Asymptotes of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
